WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:06.069
This episode of Real Engineering is brought
to you by CuriosityStream, watch over 2,400

00:00:06.069 --> 00:00:08.980
documentaries for free for 31 days at curiositystream.com/realengineering.

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:16.769
We have all been there, visiting your local
hardware store like a kid in a candy shop.

00:00:16.769 --> 00:00:21.300
You arrive at the pick and mix screw section
and you are just overcome with excitement.

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:24.480
How could you possibly pick just one type
of screw.

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:26.119
Woooooow look at all these screws.

00:00:26.119 --> 00:00:27.119
Amaaaazing.

00:00:27.119 --> 00:00:31.839
You have sultry slotted screws, rowdy robertsons,
playful phillips, oh and look at this one

00:00:31.839 --> 00:00:32.960
a hex screw.

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:34.890
You naughty polygon.

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:38.800
In your excitement you pick the largest bag
you can find and fulfill your childhood dreams

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:41.440
to buy 2 of every kind of screw.

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:43.400
Mother can’t stop you now.

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:45.510
You arrive home with the glee of a spring
lamb.

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:47.420
Oh the assembly that awaits.

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:48.500
What joy.

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:53.380
Then, to your horror, you open your toolbox
to discover you only have a fat flat head

00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:57.060
screwdriver that doesn’t even fit the flat
head screws your bought.

00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:58.190
Your life is a lie.

00:00:58.190 --> 00:01:02.030
You spent your life studying theory and never
learned practical application.

00:01:02.030 --> 00:01:04.309
You are no real engineer.

00:01:04.309 --> 00:01:09.250
Shhh it’s all going to be okay, dry those
existential crisis tears.

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:12.970
We are going to learn what all these screws
are for, and how they came into existence.

00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:19.650
The humble screw is a technology so old that
we cannot easily determine who first invented

00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:24.780
it, but the answer as best we can tell is
Archimedes who used the helical screws ability

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:29.810
to turn rotational motion into linear force
to pump water, though he probably stole the

00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:31.820
idea from the Egyptians.

00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:36.600
You can imagine how a screw works fairly simply
by viewing the cross-section at the bottom.

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:40.440
This part is essentially a wedge, and when
you force a wedge under something it will

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:41.700
lift it up.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:46.369
This wedge shape spirals all the way up the
screw, allowing that force to be applied along

00:01:46.369 --> 00:01:48.450
the full length of the screw.

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:53.190
This idea was used for centuries to pump water,
dig holes, and for pressing the shit out of

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:54.970
grapes to make some wine.

00:01:54.970 --> 00:01:58.049
Then some unknown person had a bright idea.

00:01:58.049 --> 00:02:03.810
If we can apply an opposing force, we could
create compression to hold two parts together,

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:08.310
and so someone slapped a head on one of these
screws that would press down while the threads

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:09.709
pulled upwards.

00:02:09.709 --> 00:02:12.370
Creating an incredibly useful fastener.

00:02:12.370 --> 00:02:16.790
This idea didn’t really take off until a
method of mass manufacturing them came about

00:02:16.790 --> 00:02:23.040
in 1797, when Henry Maudsley invented this
metal cutting lathe that allowed for the consistent

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:25.310
and precise cutting of screw threads.

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:31.459
He even set up a standard screw thread geometry
for his machine shop , and cut all his nuts

00:02:31.459 --> 00:02:36.090
and bolts to fit those threads, and thus the
chaos started.

00:02:36.090 --> 00:02:40.390
Several decades of differing standards resulted
in headaches far worse that your annoyance

00:02:40.390 --> 00:02:43.760
at new phone not fitting your old charging
dock.

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:50.070
Perhaps the most notable incompatibility occured
during the great boston fire of 1872 when

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:54.239
fire departments from neighbouring regions
arrived to help, only to discoverer their

00:02:54.239 --> 00:02:56.890
fire hoses didn’t fit Boston’s fire hydrants.

00:02:56.890 --> 00:02:58.030
[Maybe show XKCD comic]

00:02:58.030 --> 00:03:02.879
Most of the world has now accepted ISO metric
threads as a standard, although one country

00:03:02.879 --> 00:03:06.310
is still holding onto the inch as the standard
unit of measurement.

00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:10.500
A measurement that was legally defined as
the length of “three grains of barley, dry

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:15.819
and round, placed end to end, lengthwise”
for 8 centuries until it was redefined as

00:03:15.819 --> 00:03:17.220
25.4 millimetres.

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:22.310
Because, as we all know, the best in class
is always defined by the second best, but

00:03:22.310 --> 00:03:23.460
I digress.

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:28.130
We can now generally trust that a nut and
bolt with the same diameters will fit, even

00:03:28.130 --> 00:03:32.120
when mixing freedom units and metric, but
what’s the story with all these different

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:33.120
screw heads.

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:38.140
Why can’t we just agree on one shape so
we don’t need a toolbox full of screw drivers?

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:42.670
The simple slotted screw head was likely the
first type used as it’s easy and cheap to

00:03:42.670 --> 00:03:47.310
manufacture with a cutting tool, but is a
pretty terrible design for anything other

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:50.540
than manual screw driving into wood.

00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:54.881
Screwdrivers can slip out of sides and you
can turn the screw off its centre axis, which

00:03:54.881 --> 00:03:58.300
causes it drive into the material at an angle.

00:03:58.300 --> 00:04:03.489
This was not acceptable for mass production
methods, which as we explored previously,

00:04:03.489 --> 00:04:05.800
needed a foolproof production method.

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:11.590
So Peter Lymburner Robertson designed a manufacturing
process for this square bit screw-head.

00:04:11.590 --> 00:04:15.830
Which was designed to be easily and quickly
driven home, without danger of the screwdriver

00:04:15.830 --> 00:04:19.769
slipping out and damaging either the screw
of the workpiece.

00:04:19.769 --> 00:04:25.410
700 of these bad boys were used in the Model
T, and it saved Henry Ford about 2 hours of

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:28.500
manufacturing time on each and every vehicle.

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:33.180
Henry Ford was so happy with the design that
he wanted to licence it and manufacture them

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:38.670
himself to ensure he had a reliable and steady
supply of the fastener, but Robertson wasn’t

00:04:38.670 --> 00:04:41.170
about that money making life and said no.

00:04:41.170 --> 00:04:46.060
He continued doing this with other manufacturers
for some bizarre reason and now the design

00:04:46.060 --> 00:04:52.770
is mostly just used in Canada, and so in stepped
Henry F Phillips with his infuriating screw

00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:53.770
design.

00:04:53.770 --> 00:04:57.050
He licensed out the design to Henry Ford and
many other people.

00:04:57.050 --> 00:05:02.710
This conical cross design allowed for a single
screw driver to fit many sizes of screws,

00:05:02.710 --> 00:05:07.210
was self-centering, and was designed to cause
the screwdriver to slip out of the slot at

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:12.340
a certain torque and thus prevent workers
from over tightening it and damage the screwdriver

00:05:12.340 --> 00:05:13.669
or workpiece.

00:05:13.669 --> 00:05:18.300
It also prevents you from getting the blasted
thing out of a workpiece with even the slight

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:19.650
bit of rust.

00:05:19.650 --> 00:05:25.970
By 1939 this design was licenced to nearly
every automotive, airplane and rail manufacturer

00:05:25.970 --> 00:05:32.140
in the US, just in time for the boom in manufacturing
created by World War 2 [1] With the lend lease

00:05:32.140 --> 00:05:37.210
act, many American designs were sold to their
Allies, and thus the screw design spread even

00:05:37.210 --> 00:05:38.370
further.

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:42.590
Now that most screwdrivers can automatically
limit torque the phillips design is pretty

00:05:42.590 --> 00:05:48.100
much just a pain in the ass, so other designs
have come to the fore in more recent years.

00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:53.930
Specifically designed to stop that slipping
called cam-out, like hex screws and torx screws.

00:05:53.930 --> 00:05:59.080
Let’s compare these two types of screw to
see how their design came into existence.

00:05:59.080 --> 00:06:01.830
These two screws have the same diameter tool.

00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:05.170
Both have six points of contact between the
screw and tool.

00:06:05.170 --> 00:06:10.900
However we can see when we rotate our hex
tool it contacts the screw at a greater radius.

00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:15.820
Allowing it to applying more torque with the
same force, but the plane of contact is not

00:06:15.820 --> 00:06:20.070
perpendicular to the circle and that means
some of the force from the rotation of the

00:06:20.070 --> 00:06:24.050
screwdriver is being applied outwards radially
to the screw.

00:06:24.050 --> 00:06:25.789
This can damage both tools.

00:06:25.789 --> 00:06:30.820
With the torx screwhead the angle of contact
is near perpendicular, meaning it can applied

00:06:30.820 --> 00:06:33.660
more torque without fear of damaging the screw.

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:37.330
But ironically, even though this is a great
screwhead design.

00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:40.699
It was designed to not be used by the general
populace.

00:06:40.699 --> 00:06:44.860
It was used in Apple’s first personal computer
[2] to make it harder for the average guy

00:06:44.860 --> 00:06:48.419
with a phillips head screwdriver from opening
up the machine.

00:06:48.419 --> 00:06:53.550
But thanks to it’s design, Torx screws started
proliferating, and more and more people had

00:06:53.550 --> 00:06:55.430
the correct tool to loosen it.

00:06:55.430 --> 00:06:59.350
So someone decided to place a pin in the middle
to make it even harder.

00:06:59.350 --> 00:07:03.819
Bringing us to an age where our right to repair
is questioned and many random screw designs

00:07:03.819 --> 00:07:08.009
have appeared to make it harder for us to
take apart our gadgets . Why?

00:07:08.009 --> 00:07:10.039
Because screw you that’s why.

00:07:10.039 --> 00:07:14.270
If all these nuts are driving you crazy, you
might just be a squirrel and being a worldly

00:07:14.270 --> 00:07:18.500
squirrel you probably learn about all the
other types of squirrels around the world.

00:07:18.500 --> 00:07:21.789
There’s ones with wings, ones with cute
little ears and tails.

00:07:21.789 --> 00:07:23.919
Look at this ginger one, class.

00:07:23.919 --> 00:07:25.490
If you want to learn more about them.

